# CartWise — required environment variables. Chapter 25.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is gitignored; this file is the committed
# template and must never carry a real value.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# docker-compose.yml reads `.env` automatically from the repository root.
#
# WHICH OF THESE ARE ACTUALLY REQUIRED depends on the profile the backend runs under:
#
#   dev  (the default, and what docker-compose.yml uses) — every variable here has a fallback in
#        application-dev.yml, so the stack starts even with an empty .env. Setting them is how you
#        point dev at something other than a laptop.
#   prod — DB_URL, DB_USERNAME, DB_PASSWORD and JWT_SECRET have NO defaults in
#        application-prod.yml. An unset one stops the boot on purpose, so that a misconfigured
#        deployment fails loudly instead of quietly falling back to something reachable.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# JDBC URL. Inside Docker Compose the host is the service name (`postgres`), not localhost —
# localhost in a container means the container itself.
DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://postgres:5432/cartwise

DB_USERNAME=cartwise
DB_PASSWORD=

# Consumed by the postgres image itself to initialise the cluster on first boot. Keep
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD and DB_PASSWORD identical or the backend cannot authenticate against the
# database Compose just created for it.
POSTGRES_DB=cartwise
POSTGRES_USER=cartwise
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# HS512 signing key for JWTs. Must be at least 64 bytes — JwtProperties rejects anything shorter
# at startup, because jjwt refuses to sign with a weak key rather than doing it quietly.
#
# Generate one:
#   openssl rand -base64 64
#
# Rotating it invalidates every issued token, signing all users out. That is the intended effect
# of a rotation and the reason to do it after any suspected exposure.
JWT_SECRET=

# Token lifetime. Accepts Spring's duration syntax (24h, 30m, 7d).
JWT_EXPIRATION=24h


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated exact origins allowed to call the API. No wildcards: the API is called with an
# Authorization header, and credentialed CORS forbids `*` outright.
#
# Compose default is the frontend container's published origin. For a real deployment this is the
# public frontend URL, e.g. https://cartwise.example.com
CARTWISE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8081


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Openverse image search (Chapter 24) — OPTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Leave both blank to call anonymously, which is the normal and supported mode. The image backfill
# issues one request per catalogue category (seven for the seven categories, not one per product),
# and that fits the anonymous allowance several times over.
#
# Supplying credentials only raises the rate ceiling:
#   anonymous, verified   200/day,  20/min
#   client_credentials  10,000/day, 100/min
#
# Obtain a pair with:
#   curl -X POST https://api.openverse.org/v1/auth_tokens/register/ \
#     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
#     -d '{"name":"your-app","description":"...","email":"you@example.com"}'
OPENVERSE_CLIENT_ID=
OPENVERSE_CLIENT_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting (Chapter 25)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All optional — RateLimitProperties carries sane defaults. Set them to loosen limits for a load
# test, or tighten them under abuse. Zero or negative disables the bucket entirely, which is a
# thing you want to be able to do without a redeploy.

# Auth endpoints (login/signup), keyed per client IP.
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_CAPACITY=10
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_REFILL_SECONDS=60

# Wishlist/comparison writes, keyed per authenticated user.
RATE_LIMIT_WRITE_CAPACITY=60
RATE_LIMIT_WRITE_REFILL_SECONDS=60

# The admin image backfill, which spends a third party's quota on every call.
RATE_LIMIT_ADMIN_CAPACITY=3
RATE_LIMIT_ADMIN_REFILL_SECONDS=3600

# Affiliate click tracking (Chapter 26), keyed per client IP. Public and unauthenticated, and its
# output is the number a commercial arrangement is settled on — so fabricating clicks is the obvious
# abuse and needs no account. 30/min is far above real use and far below what faking a plausible
# traffic figure would need.
RATE_LIMIT_CLICK_CAPACITY=30
RATE_LIMIT_CLICK_REFILL_SECONDS=60


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Affiliate credentials (Chapter 26)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CartWise earns a commission when a visitor clicks through to a retailer and buys there. It does
# not process payment and does not fulfil orders — the transaction happens entirely on the
# retailer's site.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS A REAL CREDENTIAL, AND NONE IS COMMITTED ANYWHERE IN THIS REPOSITORY.
#
# The default in application.yml is the placeholder `cartwise-test-00`. It tracks nothing and earns
# nothing. Replacing it is the entire "go live" step — no code change, no rebuild of the frontend,
# nothing in the JavaScript bundle to update, because the URL is assembled server-side and the tag
# never reaches a browser.
#
# HOW TO GET A REAL ONE (a human has to do this; it cannot be automated)
#
#   Amazon Associates (amazon.in)
#     Apply at https://affiliate-program.amazon.in/. The application is reviewed, and per Amazon's
#     own help pages the account is only confirmed once it has referred THREE QUALIFYING SALES
#     within 180 DAYS of applying; miss that and the application is withdrawn (you may reapply).
#     A tracking id looks like `yoursite-21` on the India store.
#
#   Flipkart
#     Direct sign-ups have been PAUSED SINCE 5 MAY 2018. The catalogue is reachable through an
#     affiliate network instead (Cuelinks, EarnKaro, CJ, Rakuten and similar), each of which has its
#     own application. A network link does not use flipkart.com at all — it goes through the
#     network's redirector — which is why AFFILIATE_FLIPKART_SEARCH_URL exists alongside the tag.
#
#   Croma / Reliance Digital / Vijay Sales
#     No documented first-party affiliate parameter. All three are network-only, same as Flipkart.
#     Left with an empty tag AND an empty tag-param by default: an invented parameter name would
#     produce a link that looks tagged, is not, and would be described to the user as an affiliate
#     link. A false disclosure is worse than a missing one.

# LEFT COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE, unlike the empty OPENVERSE_* above. Spring's `${VAR:default}` falls
# back to the default only when the variable is ABSENT — a variable that is present and empty
# resolves to the empty string. So `AFFILIATE_AMAZON_TAG=` would not mean "use the default", it would
# mean "no tag", silently untagging every Amazon link. Uncomment a line only when you have a value
# to put after the `=`.

# AFFILIATE_AMAZON_TAG=
# AFFILIATE_AMAZON_TAG_PARAM=
# AFFILIATE_AMAZON_SEARCH_URL=

# AFFILIATE_FLIPKART_TAG=
# AFFILIATE_FLIPKART_TAG_PARAM=
# AFFILIATE_FLIPKART_SEARCH_URL=

# AFFILIATE_CROMA_TAG=
# AFFILIATE_CROMA_TAG_PARAM=
# AFFILIATE_CROMA_SEARCH_URL=

# AFFILIATE_RELIANCE_TAG=
# AFFILIATE_RELIANCE_TAG_PARAM=
# AFFILIATE_RELIANCE_SEARCH_URL=

# AFFILIATE_VIJAY_SALES_TAG=
# AFFILIATE_VIJAY_SALES_TAG_PARAM=
# AFFILIATE_VIJAY_SALES_SEARCH_URL=
